WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.670
<v Ira Glass>Fifth grade-- it was so long ago. Who can remember that far back? Two whole years.
<v Ira Glass>五年级——那已经是好久以前的事了。谁还能记得那么久远的事情呢？整整两年啊。

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:13.700
<v Kayla Hernandez>I remember all of, like, the old things. Like we used to read the book Harry Potter when it first came out. And we made curtains.
<v Kayla Hernandez>我记得所有那些旧事物。比如我们曾经在《哈利·波特》刚出版时就读过，还亲手做了窗帘。

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.930
<v Ira Glass>Harry Potter curtains.
<v Ira Glass>哈利·波特窗帘。

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:26.190
<v Kayla Hernandez>And they have new curtains now. And I look back at them, and I'm like, wow, you know? It's changed. And I wish it was still there somehow.
<v Kayla Hernandez>现在换了新窗帘了。我回想起从前，心想，哇，你知道吗？一切都变了。我真希望它还能以某种方式保留在那里。

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:42.800
<v Ira Glass>This is Kayla Hernandez, in seventh grade at the Pulaski School in Chicago. She says that she actually visits her fifth grade classroom, Room 211, and her fifth grade teacher, Mrs. Chan, fairly often, and reminisces about the past.
<v Ira Glass>这是凯拉·埃尔南德斯，芝加哥普拉斯基学校七年级的学生。她说她经常回到五年级的教室——211教室，去看望她的五年级老师陈老师，并回忆过去。

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.110
<v Kayla Hernandez>Recently I went through the shelves, and our books are still there. Like Our America.
<v Kayla Hernandez>最近我翻了翻书架，我们的书还在那里。比如《我们的美国》。

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.650
<v Ira Glass>You're talking about the book Our America?
<v Ira Glass>你是指《我们的美国》那本书吗？

00:00:51.650 --> 00:01:03.480
<v Kayla  Hernandez>Yeah. You know, reminiscing about when we used to read that book. And how it showed lots of racism.
<v Kayla  Hernandez>是的。你知道，回想起我们以前读那本书的时候。它展现了许多种族歧视的内容。

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:14.550
<v Ira Glass>Back in fifth grade, she covered her copy of the book with one of those paper book covers with a picture of NSync on it. It was her copy, though they're not allowed to write their name in the front of the books at her school.
<v Ira Glass>五年级的时候，她用那种印有“超级男孩”（NSync）组合照片的纸质书皮包着她的书。那是她自己的书，尽管在她的学校里，学生们是不允许在书的扉页写名字的。

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.810
<v Kayla Hernandez>They had numbers, and my number, I think, was like 30.
<v Kayla Hernandez>书上有编号，我想我的编号大概是30号。

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.720
<v Ira Glass>So you did find book number 30?
<v Ira Glass>所以你真的找到了30号书？

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:43.180
<v Kayla Hernandez>Yeah. I did. I saw the book and it was just there, without its paperback covering. You know, everything that was mine is not mine anymore. I think that's the hardest thing from switching to another grade, and to another classroom and to another teacher. And there's a new environment and different things to learn, and old memories to leave behind.
<v Kayla Hernandez>嗯，找到了。我看到了那本书，它就静静地躺在那里，没有了纸质书皮。你知道，曾经属于我的一切现在都不再属于我了。我觉得从一个年级转到另一个年级、换到另一个教室、换到另一位老师，这是最难接受的事情。这里有新的环境，有不同的东西要学，还有必须留下的旧回忆。

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:54.690
<v Ira       Glass>20 years from now, 30 years from now, when you try to remember back to seventh grade, what do you think you're going to remember from this year?
<v Ira       Glass>20年或30年后，当你试图回想七年级时，你觉得你还会记得这一年的什么事情？

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:58.220
<v Kayla Hernandez>I think I'll remember barely anything.
<v Kayla Hernandez>我觉得我几乎什么都不会记得了。

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:05.710
<v Ira Glass>Isn't that kind of strange, though, to think that you're going through all these experiences now that somehow are going to get wiped off the blackboard?
<v Ira Glass>但想到你现在经历的所有这些体验，到头来都会像黑板上的粉笔字一样被擦掉，这难道不奇怪吗？

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:24.550
<v Kayla Hernandez>Yeah. But I even have that experience now. Like I can't remember things from like second grade. Like some things. Like, I remember this kid, he wrote this Valentine card for me, like, you're pretty as a rose. I don't know. Something like that. But I can't remember teachers really well like I used to.
<v Kayla Hernandez>是啊。但我现在就有这种感觉。比如我无法想起二年级时的一些事情。有些事我记得，比如有个男孩给我写过一张情人节卡片，写着“你美如玫瑰”之类的话。我不知道，大概是那样吧。但我没法像过去那样清楚地记得老师们了。

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.830
<v Ira Glass>Do you feel sad about that, or is that OK?
<v Ira Glass>你对此感到难过，还是觉得这很正常？

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:41.500
<v Kayla Hernandez>I feel said about that. Because it's a part of me. It's like you don't even remember what's happened. It's kind of hard. Because it's been a part of you.
<v Kayla Hernandez>我感到难过。因为那是构成我的一部分。就像你甚至不记得发生过什么一样。这很难受，因为它曾经是你的一部分。

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:49.370
<v Ira   Glass>When I asked Kayla which friends that she wouldn't remember at all someday, it wasn't hard for her to answer.
<v Ira   Glass>当我问凯拉未来她会完全记不起哪些朋友时，她很快就回答了出来。

00:02:49.370 --> 00:03:02.020
<v Kayla Hernandez>Cynthia. I'll probably forget Alileni. I'll probably forget Diana and Maria. I'll forget Erica Sorio. I'll forget a whole bunch of people.
<v Kayla Hernandez>辛西娅。我可能还会忘了阿利莱尼。我也可能会忘了戴安娜和玛丽亚。我会忘了埃里卡·索里奥。我会忘了很多人。

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:14.930
<v Ira Glass>She's not close to these kids or anything. But as she said their names, it was like watching them vaporize or something. Someday they'd just be gone, erased from the history of her life, like they had never been there in the first place.
<v Ira Glass>她和这些孩子并不是很亲近。但当她说出这些名字时，看着他们消失，就像看着他们蒸发了一样。总有一天，他们会彻底消失，从她的人生历史中抹去，仿佛他们从来没有出现过一样。

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:29.100
<v Ira Glass>We forget most of everything, and then sometimes we go back and try to remember. And really, there is no predicting which people and places and moments we'll be able to get back. Diana and Maria? They could still make the cut.
<v Ira Glass>我们忘记了几乎所有事情，然后偶尔回头试图回想。实际上，谁也无法预料我们能找回哪些人、哪些地方和哪些瞬间。戴安娜和玛丽亚？她们也许还能留在我记忆里。

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:41.270
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, Return to Childhood. What you find and what you do not find when you go back.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天节目的主题是“重返童年”。当你回去时，你会发现什么，又会错过什么。

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:55.640
<v Ira Glass>Our show today in four acts. Act one. Once More, With Feeling. In which Jonathan Katz answers the age old question, what happens if you return to your childhood home and it's not so nice, and the current owner tries to sell it to you?
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目分为四个篇章。第一幕：再来一次，带着感情。乔纳森·卡茨回答了一个老生常谈的问题：如果你回到童年的家，却发现它不那么美好，而且现任房主还试图把它卖给你，会发生什么？

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:09.200
<v Ira Glass>Act two. Punk In a Grey Flannel Suit In which a mortgage broker discovers that his old punk band from the '70s is hot in Japan and decides to leave corporate life for a little bit and go back on tour.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕：身穿灰色法兰绒西装的朋克。一位抵押贷款经纪人发现他70年代的旧朋克乐队在日本很受欢迎，于是决定暂时离开企业生活，重回巡演之路。

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:21.690
<v Ira Glass>Act three. Ich... Bin... Ein... Mophead. A 34 year old man investigates who he was at nine years old, and learns a thing or two that he would just as soon not remember.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕：我是……那个……拖把头。一位34岁的男子调查了自己9岁时是个什么样的人，并学到了一些他宁愿不去记住的事情。

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:32.440
<v Ira Glass>Act four. Every day I forget something else. A ten year old explains memory with the help of novelist Nicholson Baker. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕：我每天都在遗忘。一位10岁的小女孩在小说家尼科尔森·贝克的帮助下解释记忆。请继续收听。

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:40.810
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Once More, With Feeling.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：再来一次，带着感情。

00:04:40.810 --> 00:05:07.770
<v Ira Glass>You may have heard of this series on Public Radio called Lost and Found Sound, which looks for old tapes to put on the radio. Well, one of the producers who works with that series, Valerie Velardi, knew a man named Jonathan Katz who loves old tapes. Loves them so much that he says that it just puts him in a good mood just to be around audio equipment. Naturally they got in touch. Katz is also a kind of minor celebrity in his own right. He did a TV series for a while about a cartoon psychiatrist called Dr. Katz.
<v Ira Glass>你可能听说过公共广播电台的一档节目叫《失而复得之声》，专门搜寻老磁带并进行广播。该系列的一位制片人瓦莱丽·维拉尔迪认识一位热爱老磁带的男士，名叫乔纳森·卡茨。他热爱到什么程度呢？他说只要待在音响设备旁边，心情就会变好。顺理成章地，他们取得了联系。卡茨本人也是一位小有名气的人物。他曾出演过一部关于卡通精神病医生的电视剧，叫《卡茨医生》。

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:21.690
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, Lost and Found Sound had Katz sit down with another one of the producers, a guy named Jay Allison, to play some of these old tapes of Katz's family. And after they did that, Katz then took his little journey into his childhood a step further.
<v Ira Glass>总之，《失而复得之声》让卡茨与另一位制片人杰伊·阿利森坐在一起，播放了一些卡茨家庭的老磁带。在那之后，卡茨将他的童年之旅又向前推进了一步。

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:28.920
<v Ira Glass>But I'm getting ahead of the story. Here's a few minutes first of him going through his old childhood tapes with Jay Allison.
<v Ira Glass>但我说得有点超前了。先让他和杰伊·阿利森一起翻看童年磁带的几分钟片段。

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:35.840
<v Jonathan Katz>K, I'm going to put the headphones on and do some serious time traveling. OK, hit play.
<v Jonathan Katz>好了，我要戴上耳机开始一次严肃的时光旅行。OK，播放。

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.050
<v Young Jonathan    Katz>[SINGING]
<v Young Jonathan    Katz>[歌唱]

00:05:41.050 --> 00:06:09.330
<v Jonathan  Katz>I think even as a kid I knew how cheap tape was. If this was recorded today, there would be phones in the background. Somebody would be getting a fax. Somebody else would be checking their email. The TV would be on. It's kind of sad. Can't hear a kid breathe like this.
<v Jonathan  Katz>我想即使是个孩子，我也知道磁带有多便宜。如果这是今天录的，背景里肯定会有电话声。有人在收传真，有人在查邮件，电视开着。这真有点可悲。没法像这样听到一个孩子呼吸的声音。

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:15.410
<v Young  Jonathan  Katz>Boy, am I wasting tape.
<v Young  Jonathan  Katz>天哪，我真是浪费磁带。

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:40.280
<v Rabbi>Now it gives me great pleasure, on behalf of our entire congregation, to express our very good wishes to Mr. Sidney R. Katz and to Mrs. Katz upon the Bar Mitzvah this morning of their son Jonathan.
<v Rabbi>现在，我代表我们的整个会众，非常荣幸地向西德尼·R·卡茨先生和卡茨夫人致以美好的祝愿，祝贺他们的儿子乔纳森今天早上的受戒仪式。

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:45.430
<v Young       Jonathan Katz>To the ten thousands of the clans of Israel--
<v Young       Jonathan Katz>致以色列部落的万千族人——

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:46.410
<v Jonathan Katz>That's me.
<v Jonathan Katz>那是我的声音。

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.320
<v Young Jonathan Katz>Verily I give you good doctrine--
<v Young Jonathan Katz>我确实给予你们美好的教义——

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.080
<v Jonathan Katz>I said "verily."
<v Jonathan Katz>我说的是“确实”。

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.260
<v Young Jonathan Katz>Bring us back to thee, oh Lord--
<v Young Jonathan Katz>主啊，带我们回到你身边——

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:55.350
<v Jonathan Katz>Lohd.
<v Jonathan Katz>主。

00:06:55.350 --> 00:07:01.180
<v Young  Jonathan  Katz>Renew our days as of old. [SINGING IN HEBREW]
<v Young  Jonathan  Katz>让我们如旧日般重焕新生。[用希伯来语歌唱]

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:21.990
<v Jonathan Katz>I have a New York accent in Hebrew, which is pretty tough. Until I did my HBO special, this was the most money I ever got paid to perform live. Just going to move it ahead a little bit, just want to make sure-- whoa. Sorry about that.
<v Jonathan Katz>我用希伯来语说话时竟然有纽约口音，这太难了。在做我的HBO特辑之前，这是我现场表演拿到的最高报酬。我往前快进一点，我想确保——哇。抱歉。

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:25.520
<v Young  Jonathan Katz>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] We're desperate.
<v Young  Jonathan Katz>[听不清] 我们很绝望。

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.220
<v Girl>Doesn't sound like too much fun.
<v Girl>听起来不太有趣。

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:32.070
<v Young Jonathan Katz>Nah. What are we having for dinner?
<v Young Jonathan Katz>没。我们晚餐吃什么？

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:32.870
<v Girl>Eatin' food.
<v Girl>吃食物。

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.210
<v Young  Jonathan  Katz>What kind of eatin' food?
<v Young  Jonathan  Katz>什么样的食物？

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:39.510
<v Girl>The kind that people eat.
<v Girl>那种人们吃的食物。

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.580
<v Young Jonathan Katz>OK. Let's hear this recording, OK?
<v Young Jonathan Katz>OK。听听这段录音，好吗？

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:52.160
<v Jonathan  Katz>Oh, man. I have not changed one bit. That's what life is like at my house. We live, we videotape, and then we watch what we just did.
<v Jonathan  Katz>天哪，我一点都没变。这就是我家里的生活状态。我们生活，我们录像，然后回看我们刚才做的事。

00:07:52.160 --> 00:08:10.740
<v Jonathan  Katz>And I have this obnoxious habit now with my daughter and her friends, both daughters. They can just be sitting around with a bunch of friends and I walk in with a tape recorder, and I go, "OK, talent show!" You know, which if you're 17, is pretty uncomfortable.
<v Jonathan  Katz>现在我和女儿还有她的朋友们在一起时有一个令人讨厌的习惯。她们可能和一群朋友坐在一起，我拿着录音机走进去说：“OK，才艺表演！”你知道，如果你17岁，这会让人非常尴尬。

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:22.570
<v Ira   Glass>Jonathan Katz now joins us from his home studio in Boston. Jonathan, how much taping do you do over the course of the week?
<v Ira   Glass>乔纳森·卡茨现在从波士顿的家庭工作室加入我们。乔纳森，你一周大概录多少时间的音？

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:36.230
<v Jonathan  Katz>Talent show! You know, I pretty much am always rolling. I'm recording something or listening to something. I'm surrounded by my past on audiotape.
<v Jonathan  Katz>才艺表演！你知道，我基本上一直在录。我总是在录音或者听东西。我的过去都被磁带包围着。

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.940
<v Ira Glass>See, but how many hours of tape do you have there in your house?
<v Ira Glass>但是，你家里到底有多少小时的磁带？

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:46.500
<v Jonathan Katz>I would say-- you know, and it's very hard for me to give you an accurate estimate. But I would say 5,000 hours.
<v Jonathan Katz>我想说——你知道，给我一个准确的估计很难。但我会说，5000个小时。

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.500
<v Ira Glass>And how much of it have you actually gone back to?
<v Ira Glass>你真正回头去听过多少？

00:08:49.500 --> 00:09:00.610
<v Jonathan Katz>Well, if I'm sitting in my office in my house writing something, rather than going downstairs and having a cup of coffee, I put on an old reel-to-reel tape and see what I find.
<v Jonathan Katz>嗯，如果我坐在书房里写东西，而不是下楼去喝杯咖啡，我就会放一盘老磁带，看看我能发现什么。

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:05.330
<v Ira Glass>Let's play one of the recent recordings you've made. Let me just get this queued up.
<v Ira Glass>让我们播放一段你最近的录音。我来把它调出来。

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:10.670
<v Jonathan Katz>Now when I see a picture hanging on the wall, it just reminds me of what could have been
<v Jonathan Katz>现在当我看到墙上挂着一张照片，它只会让我想起曾经可能发生的一切。

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:12.570
<v Jonathan's Daughter>Same with me. That's what I was going to say.
<v Jonathan's Daughter>我也是。我正想说这个。

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:17.970
<v Jonathan Katz>You know? And I still hope that maybe someday we can get together--
<v Jonathan Katz>你知道吗？我仍然希望有一天我们能聚在一起——

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:19.140
<v Ira Glass>Same with me.
<v Ira Glass>我也是。

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.350
<v Jonathan Katz>And make up for lost time.
<v Jonathan Katz>弥补错失的时间。

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:24.230
<v Jonathan's  Daughter>Same with me.
<v Jonathan's  Daughter>我也是。

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:24.420
<v Jonathan's  Daughter>[SINGING]
<v Jonathan's  Daughter>[歌唱]

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:30.510
<v Jonathan Katz>And thinking of the ways that I could make you stay--
<v Jonathan Katz>想着我可以让你留下的各种方式——

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:36.560
<v Ira  Glass>It's an odd choice of subject for a song to sing with your nine year old daughter.
<v Ira  Glass>作为一个和9岁女儿合唱的歌曲主题，这选择有点奇怪。

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:43.330
<v Jonathan Katz>Yeah. Well, I mean, you could have "The Wheels on the Bus Go Round and Round" only so many times. You know?
<v Jonathan Katz>是啊。我是说，《巴士轮子转呀转》那首歌就算再好听，也不能总是循环播放，你懂吗？

00:09:43.330 --> 00:10:02.940
<v Ira   Glass>After digging up those old tapes and talking about them, you decided to get together with your sister Phyllis and return to your childhood home in Brooklyn. And it was it Sheepshead Bay?
<v Ira   Glass>在翻出那些旧磁带并谈论它们之后，你决定和你姐姐菲利斯一起回布鲁克林的童年故居。那是希普斯黑德湾吗？

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:10.250
<v Jonathan   Katz>Sheepshead Bay was where we lived. My earliest childhood memories were of our house in Sheepshead Bay.
<v Jonathan   Katz>我们住在希普斯黑德湾。我最早的童年记忆就是关于我们在希普斯黑德湾的房子的。

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.460
<v Ira Glass>And how had it been since you'd been back?
<v Ira Glass>自从你回去过之后，感觉如何？

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:18.400
<v Jonathan  Katz>I don't think I'd been there since the 1950s. It was a dirt road the last time I was there.
<v Jonathan  Katz>我想我从20世纪50年代起就没回去过。我上次去那儿时，那还是条土路。

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.120
<v Ira Glass>No.
<v Ira Glass>不是吧。

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:20.230
<v Jonathan Katz>Yep.
<v Jonathan Katz>真的。

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:24.730
<v Ira Glass>A part of Brooklyn was a dirt road in the 1950s?
<v Ira Glass>50年代的布鲁克林还有土路？

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.010
<v Jonathan Katz>It's true. We lived on a dirt road.
<v Jonathan Katz>这是真的。我们当时就住在一条土路上。

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:32.240
<v Phyllis>Why don't we turn right at Avenue 1 and go past your school to Ocean Avenue, OK?
<v Phyllis>我们为什么不在第一大道右转，经过你的学校到海洋大道呢，好吗？

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.820
<v Ira Glass>Just explain the setup in the car when you went back to Brooklyn. Who was there?
<v Ira Glass>解释一下你们回布鲁克林时车里的情况。都有谁？

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:42.940
<v Jonathan  Katz>I was driving with my sister Phyllis and an engineer.
<v Jonathan  Katz>我和我姐姐菲利斯还有一位工程师。

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:46.220
<v Phyllis>God. Marilyn Maybloom lived down whatever avenue it was.
<v Phyllis>天哪。玛丽琳·梅布卢姆就住在不管哪条大道上。

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:57.960
<v Ira Glass>So you drove back to the old neighborhood, and you drive to a series of different houses that you had lived in. What was it like to knock on these doors of these different houses, trying to talk your way in?
<v Ira Glass>所以你们开车回到了旧街区，去了你们住过的不同房子。敲开这些房子的门，试图进去看看，是什么感觉？

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:07.620
<v Jonathan  Katz>That was fun. And I think Phyllis has sort of a distorted view of my celebrity. That it was going to impress people, the fact that I used to be a cartoon.
<v Jonathan  Katz>那很有趣。我觉得菲利斯对我的名气有一种扭曲的看法。她觉得我曾经是个卡通人物，这会让人们印象深刻。

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:14.790
<v Ira Glass>Right. You say to one person after another, I used to have this TV show. And not a one of them seems to have watched that show.
<v Ira Glass>对。你对一个人接着一个人说，我曾经有一个电视节目。结果没一个人看过那个节目。

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:16.340
<v Resident  1>Who are you?
<v Resident  1>你是谁？

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:28.290
<v Jonathan Katz>I'm Jonathan Katz. We used to live in this building. And I had a TV show called Dr. Katz remember a few years back? And I'm here with my sister Phyllis out of sentiment. And we lived on the fifth floor of this building.
<v Jonathan Katz>我是乔纳森·卡茨。我们以前住在这栋楼里。我几年前做过一个叫《卡茨医生》的电视节目，还记得吗？我是和我姐姐菲利斯出于怀旧来的。我们以前住在这栋楼的五楼。

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.360
<v Resident 1>Well, if you'll excuse me, we're a little busy.
<v Resident 1>好吧，请原谅，我们有点忙。

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.360
<v Phyllis>I'm sorry. [INAUDIBLE] But we'll just go up. Thank you.
<v Phyllis>抱歉。[听不清] 不过我们还是上去吧。谢谢。

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:43.500
<v Jonathan  Katz>I guess the most amazing was this guy out of 88th Street. When he announced that he couldn't let us in because he's on an international call.
<v Jonathan  Katz>我想最让人吃惊的是88街的那个人。当他宣布不能让我们进去，因为他正在打国际长途时。

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.230
<v Ira Glass>I know. It seems so archaic.
<v Ira Glass>我知道。这看起来太古老了。

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:52.200
<v Jonathan  Katz>Like he was the first person to make contact with somebody from another country on the phone.
<v Jonathan  Katz>就像他是世界上第一个通过电话联系上外国的人一样。

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:53.000
<v Resident 2>May I help you?
<v Resident 2>有什么可以帮你的吗？

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.230
<v Jonathan Katz>Oh, yes. Hi.
<v Jonathan Katz>噢，是的。嗨。

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:59.930
<v Resident  2>I'm on an international call, so please be quick. What is this about?
<v Resident  2>我正在打国际长途，所以请快点。这是关于什么的？

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:04.040
<v Phyllis>We used to live here in 1966 and we wanted to see the apartment?
<v Phyllis>我们1966年住在这里，我们想看看公寓？

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:06.940
<v Resident 2>No. I'm speaking to my wife in Switzerland.
<v Resident 2>不行。我正在和我在瑞士的妻子通话。

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:08.970
<v Phyllis>If we were to come back, would it be possible?
<v Phyllis>如果我们要回来，有可能吗？

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.890
<v Resident 2>Yeah, possibly.
<v Resident 2>嗯，或许吧。

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:15.320
<v Jonathan Katz>Sorry to bother you.
<v Jonathan Katz>抱歉打扰了。

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:23.420
<v Phyllis>Everyone makes an international call. It's something to show off about, in his mind.
<v Phyllis>每个人都会打国际长途。在他看来，这是一种可以炫耀的东西。

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:32.500
<v Ira Glass>You finally get into one of the houses. Was there a part of you that was surprised that-- like, in some way, that other people were living in the house?
<v Ira Glass>你终于进到了其中一间房子里。有没有一部分的你对此感到惊讶——比如，居然有其他人在你曾经的家里生活？

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:42.330
<v Jonathan Katz>Oh, I was shocked. I expected to see our furniture and oddly enough, my parents, who were worried sick. Where have we been?
<v Jonathan Katz>噢，我被震惊了。我本以为会看到我们的家具，奇怪的是，还有我那担心得要命的父母。我们去哪儿了？

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:47.420
<v Ira  Glass>But of course, in a way. Because that's the way it looked the last time you were there.
<v Ira  Glass>但这当然是必然的。因为那是你上次在那儿时的样子。

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:59.320
<v Jonathan  Katz>Right. I sort of think of my homes, in Brooklyn especially, as the sets of sitcoms. You know, that they're frozen in time.
<v Jonathan  Katz>对。我有点把我的家，尤其是布鲁克林的家，当成了情景喜剧的布景。你知道，它们的时间是凝固的。

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:16.990
<v Jonathan  Katz>But instead we found this guy, Frank Mendez. And the reason he let us in wasn't his sentimentality. It's that it's on the market for $439,000. And it's a dump.
<v Jonathan  Katz>但相反，我们遇到了这个人，弗兰克·门德斯。他让我们进去的原因并非感伤。是因为房子正在以43.9万美元挂牌出售。而且这房子烂透了。

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:23.460
<v Jonathan  Katz>He was very hospitable. He showed us around. We were in the room where we-- Phillis and I shared a room until we moved to Manhattan.
<v Jonathan  Katz>他非常热情。他带我们到处看了看。我们在那个房间里——菲利斯和我在搬到曼哈顿之前共用的房间。

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.890
<v Phyllis>We used to live here.
<v Phyllis>我们以前住在这儿。

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:26.790
<v Frank Mendez>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] and see it.
<v Frank Mendez>[听不清] 看看它。

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:31.360
<v Phyllis>In 1949. Where's the bathroom you got locked in? It should be over here, eh?
<v Phyllis>1949年。你被锁在里面的那个厕所在哪？应该是在这儿吧，呃？

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.580
<v Jonathan Katz>Yeah, when I was a kid, I got locked in the bathroom. They had to call the fire department.
<v Jonathan Katz>是的，我小时候被锁在厕所里。他们不得不叫消防队。

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:36.880
<v Phyllis>There's your bathroom.
<v Phyllis>那是你的厕所。

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:40.180
<v Jonathan Katz>She remembered it as cozy. I remembered it as claustrophobic.
<v Jonathan Katz>她记得那很温馨。我记得那很幽闭恐惧。

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:45.660
<v Ira Glass>Let's talk about how you got along with your sister on this trip. I want to play you a piece of tape.
<v Ira Glass>聊聊你们在旅途中相处得如何。我想给你听一段磁带。

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:48.960
<v Phyllis>We just passed our old school. There's a house similar to this--
<v Phyllis>我们刚经过我们的老学校。这里有一所房子和那个很像——

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:50.460
<v Jonathan Katz>Which school, though? Did I go there?
<v Jonathan Katz>哪所学校？我去过吗？

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.340
<v Phyllis>130. But you wouldn't remember that either.
<v Phyllis>130号。但你也记不得那个了。

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.010
<v Jonathan Katz>Do you want to go by there on the way back?
<v Jonathan Katz>你回来的路上想经过那儿吗？

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:56.550
<v Phyllis>Yeah, maybe. We'll see.
<v Phyllis>是啊，或许吧。我们到时候看。

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.260
<v Jonathan Katz>Let's head for our mutual memories first.
<v Jonathan Katz>我们先去我们的共同记忆点吧。

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:02.540
<v Phyllis>But all these houses, there were beautiful private houses here, are not there.
<v Phyllis>但所有这些房子，这里曾有过漂亮的私人住宅，现在都不在了。

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:19.340
<v Jonathan   Katz>You know, I think if you dissect the dialogue, you can see that neither of us are really taking in what the other person is saying. And I'm pretty much making jokes, and whether or not she hears them, even, forget about gets them--
<v Jonathan   Katz>你知道，我认为如果你剖析这些对话，你会发现我们都没有真正听进去对方在说什么。我基本上一直在开玩笑，不管她有没有听进去，更别说听懂了——

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:23.540
<v Phyllis>Where's the theater, is it still here? Do you remember how much it used to cost?
<v Phyllis>剧院在哪，还在吗？你还记得以前要花多少钱吗？

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:30.250
<v Jonathan Katz>Can you sound a little younger and less Jewish when you ask that question?
<v Jonathan Katz>问这个问题的时候，你能不能声音听起来年轻一点，别那么犹太腔？

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:33.130
<v Phyllis>That's strange.
<v Phyllis>这真奇怪。

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:45.890
<v Jonathan Katz>We're very competitive. And especially about our pasts. It's almost like revisionists. She wants her version of the past to be the one that's perceived as the truth.
<v Jonathan Katz>我们竞争心很强。尤其是关于我们的过去。简直像历史修正主义者。她希望她的过去版本是被视为真相的那一个。

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:50.630
<v Ira Glass>What's the difference between your sister's version of the past and your version of the past?
<v Ira Glass>你和你姐姐的过去版本有什么区别？

00:14:50.630 --> 00:15:04.280
<v Jonathan  Katz>I believe that there was a second World War. No. I think that her version-- the big difference was that she and I got along when we kids.
<v Jonathan  Katz>我相信有第二次世界大战。不。我想她的版本——最大的区别在于她认为我们小时候关系很好。

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:06.640
<v Ira Glass>She thinks that you got along?
<v Ira Glass>她觉得你们关系很好？

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:11.440
<v Jonathan Katz>Yeah. She thinks that we were a happier family than I think we were.
<v Jonathan Katz>是啊。她觉得我们是一个比我认为的更幸福的家庭。

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.700
<v Ira  Glass>I wonder if just that she was happier.
<v Ira  Glass>我在想是不是因为她当时更幸福。

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:25.480
<v Jonathan Katz>That's possible. It's a nice interpretation.
<v Jonathan Katz>有可能。这是一个不错的解读。

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:33.810
<v Jonathan Katz>I expected to find some evidence of a happy childhood. When in fact I couldn't really get along with my sister.
<v Jonathan Katz>我本想寻找幸福童年的证据。而事实上，我和我姐姐根本相处不来。

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:57.410
<v Ira Glass>You know what's so strange about this trip that you took back to the neighborhood, is you're really dealing with the most potent psychological material possible. You're going back to the place where you grew up. You haven't been there in decades. You'd think it would bring back a flood of memories and feelings, and nothing happens.
<v Ira Glass>你知道，你这次重回旧街区的旅行最奇怪的地方在于，你是在处理最具潜在心理影响的素材。你回到了你成长的地方。你几十年没去过那里了。你本以为这会唤起如潮水般的记忆和感受，结果什么都没发生。

00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:26.520
<v Jonathan Katz>It is odd. Well, you know, it's a little bit like this TV show that I made. And I'm not trying to change the subject. I did a TV show recently about-- it was called Alpha Force. And the premise was, it was six guys living in a bunker underneath Utah, all specialists in some field. I was an electronics specialist. And we were set up by the government to do covert operations in foreign countries.
<v Jonathan Katz>确实很奇怪。嗯，你知道，这有点像我做的那个电视节目。我不是想转移话题。我最近做了一个节目，叫《阿尔法力量》。前提是六个家伙住在一起，在犹他州的一个地堡里，每个人都是某个领域的专家。我是电子专家。我们由政府安置，在国外执行秘密行动。

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:44.310
<v Jonathan Katz>But no one called. We're just in the bunker. No one's calling. So I suggested we start a book club. And that's kind of where we are now, at this piece.
<v Jonathan Katz>但没人打电话。我们只是待在地堡里。没人打来。所以我建议我们成立一个读书俱乐部。这就是我们现在在这个片段里的状态。

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:50.660
<v Ira   Glass>You didn't get what you wanted out of your trip back home.
<v Ira   Glass>你在回家的旅行中没有得到你想要的。

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:54.790
<v Jonathan  Katz>No.
<v Jonathan  Katz>没有。

00:16:54.790 --> 00:17:13.170
<v Ira Glass>What did you want? You said earlier, a part of what you were looking for was just some sign about whether things were happy when you were a kid. What would you have heard on these tapes, or seen in the old neighborhood, that would have given you it?
<v Ira Glass>你想要什么？你刚才说过，你寻找的一部分原因只是为了找个迹象，看看你小时候是否真的幸福。如果是这些磁带里听到的，或者是旧街区里看到的，什么能给你这个答案呢？

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:44.140
<v Jonathan Katz>You know, if I had been able to rip away at the wallpaper in that house, and see the words written by me-- "Sweet God, life is so good." I mean, I look at my 18 year old daughter, and I see how unbelievably alive and happy she is. And I guess that's the feeling that I'm looking for.
<v Jonathan Katz>你知道，如果我能撕掉那房子的壁纸，看到我自己写的字——“亲爱的上帝，生活如此美好。”我是说，我看着我18岁的女儿，看到她是多么令人难以置信地充满活力和快乐。我想那可能就是我正在寻找的感觉。

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:46.080
<v Ira Glass>You mean, when you listen back through these tapes?
<v Ira Glass>你是说当你回头听这些磁带的时候？

00:17:46.080 --> 00:18:12.170
<v Jonathan Katz>Yeah. That I'm looking for evidence that I, too used to be that happy and alive. You know, looking at a black and white photograph of that house with my father and my mother, and me and my cousins and my sister sitting on the steps might be more potent than actually sitting on those steps.
<v Jonathan Katz>是的。我是在寻找证据，证明我也曾那样快乐和充满活力。你知道，看着那张我和父母、我和堂兄弟姐妹还有姐姐坐在台阶上的黑白照片，可能比实际坐在那些台阶上更有力量。

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:30.710
<v Ira  Glass>Comedian Jonathan Katz.
<v Ira  Glass>喜剧演员乔纳森·卡茨。

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:41.520
<v Ira Glass>Act Two. Punk In a Grey Flannel Suit.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕：身穿灰色法兰绒西装的朋克。

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:51.980
<v Ira Glass>David Philp is the president of a mortgage brokerage firm in Beverly Hills. As you might imagine, in Beverly Hills, they handle rather large mortgages. He dresses neatly in beautiful clothes. He has clean-cut hair.
<v Ira Glass>戴维·菲尔普是比弗利山庄一家抵押贷款经纪公司的总裁。正如你所想的，在比弗利山庄，他们处理的是相当大的抵押贷款。他穿着整齐，服装考究。发型一丝不苟。

00:18:51.980 --> 00:19:16.630
<v Ira Glass>But in the 1970s, in his native England, he was in a punk band called the Automatics, which was never really a big commercial success, but known and respected in the history of the punk movement there by people who care about these kinds of things. And last year, through an odd set of connections, he ended up revisiting his teenage years for the first time by going back on tour in a version of his band in Japan. Here is how something like that happens.
<v Ira Glass>但在20世纪70年代，在他家乡英国，他是一个叫“自动装置”的朋克乐队成员，这支乐队在商业上从未取得过巨大成功，但在那里朋克运动的历史中，被那些关心这类事情的人所熟知和尊重。而去年，通过一系列奇妙的人脉，他终于通过在日本重回他当年的乐队巡演，第一次重温了他的少年时代。这就是这种事发生的过程。

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:35.780
<v David Philp>I mentioned it to a client. And I said, well, I played in a punk group when I was a kid. And he said, oh, really? And he's interested. And then the next day, he sent me a copy of an eBay auction, and said, is this you?
<v David Philp>我向一位客户提到过。我说，好吧，我小时候在一个朋克乐队演奏。他说，哦，真的吗？他很感兴趣。第二天，他发给我一份eBay拍卖的链接，说，这是你吗？

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:46.430
<v David Philp>It was. And I watched this auction. I watched the price shoot through the roof. And then I began to realize, wait a minute! I'm collectible.
<v David Philp>确实是我。我看着这场拍卖。我看着价格直线上升。然后我开始意识到，等一下！我很有收藏价值。

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:51.870
<v Ira   Glass>Let's get down to brass tacks here. How much were you guys?
<v Ira   Glass>让我们开门见山吧。你们卖了多少钱？

00:19:51.870 --> 00:20:21.560
<v David Philp>I think that one actually went at $48. I particularly liked looking at all those sort of other groups that were going at $0.25. You know, music business offerings along punk lines that I thought, what a load of nonsense, at the time. And it was good to see that their records weren't valued years later. I mean, it was--
<v David Philp>我觉得那一盘成交价是48美元。我特别喜欢看其他那些成交价只有0.25美元的音乐组合。你知道，当时那些朋克路线的音乐产品，我当时觉得，那真是胡扯。很高兴看到多年后他们的唱片并不值钱。我是说，这——

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:23.880
<v Ira Glass>Wow. That history came out on the right side.
<v Ira Glass>哇。历史站在了正确的一边。

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:30.340
<v David  Philp>Yes. That there is a sort of Darwinism in record-collecting.
<v David  Philp>是的。唱片收藏界也存在一种达尔文主义。

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:33.830
<v Ira Glass>What happened next?
<v Ira Glass>接下来发生了什么？

00:20:33.830 --> 00:21:00.610
<v David  Philp>I went to go see Ricky, the drummer. And Ricky collected everything. And he very kindly lent me these two scrapbooks. So I took pictures and things out of there. I just had a friend put it up on a website. And then I got an email from Fifi in Japan, saying, oh, I play in a Japanese punk rock band, and--
<v David  Philp>我去找了鼓手瑞奇。瑞奇什么都收集。他非常慷慨地把这两本剪贴簿借给了我。所以我把里面的照片之类的东西拿出来。我让一个朋友把这些放到了网站上。然后我收到了来自日本菲菲的一封邮件，说，哦，我在一个日本朋克摇滚乐队演奏，你的唱片改变了我的人生。

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:02.470
<v Ira Glass>Fifi's a name of a person?
<v Ira Glass>菲菲是一个人的名字？

00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:05.720
<v David  Philp>Yes. --and your record changed my life.
<v David  Philp>是的。——你的唱片改变了我的人生。

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:06.800
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:30.970
<v David Philp>And he found out through the website that there was an unreleased album. So he asked if he put me in touch with Toshio Iijima of Base Records. We struck up a deal. And then they said, well, would you come over here and play some gigs to, you know, promote?
<v David Philp>他通过网站发现有一张未发行的专辑。所以他问如果他联系基地唱片公司的Toshio Iijima，他可以吗。我们谈成了交易。然后他们说，好吧，你愿不愿意过来做几场演出，你知道，进行推广？

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:38.760
<v Ira        Glass>So you go to tour. How old are you at that point?
<v Ira        Glass>所以你去巡演。那时候你多大？

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:40.690
<v David Philp>45.
<v David Philp>45岁。

00:21:40.690 --> 00:21:44.000
<v Ira  Glass>A little bit of grey hair coming in, perhaps?
<v Ira  Glass>也许有点白头发了？

00:21:44.000 --> 00:22:12.190
<v David Philp>A little bit of grey hair coming in. And I really wasn't sure whether I'd still be able to do it. Because I hadn't played those songs in 22 years. You know? Not in my shower. Not to anyone. I mean, prior to being married-- I mean, I remember dating women for a year who never knew that I played. Had ever played.
<v David Philp>有点白头发了。我真的不确定我还能不能做到。因为我已经有22年没弹过那些歌了。你知道吗？连在浴室里都没弹过。没给任何人弹过。我是说，结婚前——我记得和女性约会了一年，她们都不知道我玩过乐队。从来没玩过。

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:13.420
<v Ira Glass>It wouldn't even come up?
<v Ira Glass>甚至提都没提过？

00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:20.920
<v David   Philp>Wouldn't come up, really. I mean, I'd have a guitar hanging around, but lots of other guys did, too.
<v David   Philp>没提过，真的。我是说，我家里一直有把吉他，但很多其他男人也有。

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:23.550
<v Ira Glass>Would you ever pick up the guitar and play for yourself?
<v Ira Glass>你有没有试过拿起吉他为自己弹奏？

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:28.400
<v David Philp>Yes. I wrote a lot of songs for my dog during this period.
<v David Philp>有。这段时间我为我的狗写了很多歌。

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:30.690
<v Ira Glass>Really? Some of the titles would be--
<v Ira Glass>真的？有些歌名会是——

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:33.820
<v David Philp>"We're Going to the Park" was a big favorite.
<v David Philp>《我们要去公园》是最受欢迎的。

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:38.370
<v Ira Glass>To be followed by that hit, "Who's A Good Boy?"
<v Ira Glass>随后是那首热门曲目，《谁是个乖狗狗？》

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:40.760
<v David Philp>"Oh, What A Good Boy," is actually--
<v David Philp>《噢，多乖的狗狗》，其实是——

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:41.840
<v Ira Glass>It is?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:51.660
<v David Philp>[PANTS] What a, what a good boy!
<v David Philp>[喘气声] 多乖，多乖的狗狗！

00:22:51.660 --> 00:23:03.520
<v David Philp>So we went over there October the sixth. I took my wife. Which possibly was a miscalculation, but-- No, it was a good thing to take my wife, because--
<v David Philp>所以我们在10月6日去了那里。我带上了我妻子。这可能是一个失误，但——不，带上我妻子是件好事，因为——

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:06.170
<v Ira Glass>You were approached by dozens of teenage girls?
<v Ira Glass>你被几十个十几岁的女孩搭讪了？

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:07.970
<v David Philp>I was getting stopped on the street.
<v David Philp>我走在街上被人拦住。

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:11.110
<v Ira  Glass>So what happened the first night you went onstage?
<v Ira  Glass>你第一晚登台时发生了什么？

00:23:11.110 --> 00:24:04.280
<v David Philp>Well, there was just the announcement, the light, and sort of a moment's silence, which lasted forever. And then sort of-- out at the back I heard the opening riff of "When the Tanks Roll Over Poland." And there was just this whole ignition of energy from the club, in front. And all these kids just started going mad. And it just clicked right in.
<v David Philp>嗯，主持人刚宣布完，灯光亮起，出现了一阵沉默，仿佛持续了很久。然后——在后面我听到了《坦克碾过波兰时》的开场连复段。台下的俱乐部瞬间爆发出了巨大的能量。孩子们都疯了。这感觉一下子就对上了。

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:16.020
<v David Philp>It felt like I was in an Automatics cover band or something like that. Because it was so long ago, I didn't feel that association as the writer. Because I wrote the material and all that. I didn't have that association as the writer anymore.
<v David Philp>感觉就像我在一个翻唱“自动装置”乐队的乐队里演奏一样。因为那已经是好久以前的事了，我不再有那种作为创作者的关联感。虽然那些歌是我写的。我不再有那种关联感了。

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:24.180
<v Ira Glass>See, but I would wonder, if as you sing the songs, the conviction of the writing returns to you, and you remember all the feelings of it. Did that happen?
<v Ira Glass>看，但我很好奇，当你演唱这些歌曲时，创作时的信念有没有回到你身上，你是否记起了当时所有的感受？那发生了吗？

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:53.140
<v David  Philp>There's a muscle memory that was there. You know, the movements are all locked in the lyrics and the beat and the parts. And as I played them, they all started to come out, and it was just like being a marionette or something. Here you punch the air. There you sort of bring it, remind the drummer to come down. And there you point at the guitarist for the solo.
<v David  Philp>有一种肌肉记忆就在那里。你知道，动作都锁在歌词、节奏和伴奏里了。当我弹奏它们时，一切都自然而然地出来了，就像个提线木偶一样。在这里你要挥动空气，那里你要带一下节奏提醒鼓手，那里你要指向吉他手让他独奏。

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.680
<v Ira  Glass>Had you forgotten the thrill of being onstage?
<v Ira  Glass>你有没有忘记过在舞台上的那种快感？

00:24:56.680 --> 00:25:05.330
<v David Philp>Yes. I'd forgotten what it was to have the audience right there.
<v David Philp>是的。我忘记了那种观众就在台下的感觉。

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:12.990
<v Ira  Glass>Before this, had you ever performed a punk show sober?
<v Ira  Glass>在此之前，你有过清醒地表演朋克秀吗？

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:24.240
<v David  Philp>Never. Well, unless I was taking the antibiotics.
<v David  Philp>从来没有。除非我在服用抗生素。

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:27.570
<v Ira Glass>So much information contained in such a brief sentence.
<v Ira Glass>如此简短的一句话里包含了太多的信息。

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:36.530
<v David  Philp>No, it actually was one of the great paradoxes, really, I suppose. That it was great to do it sober.
<v David  Philp>不，这实际上是一个伟大的悖论，我想。清醒着表演感觉真的很棒。

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:41.370
<v Ira  Glass>Were there moments onstage where you felt your age? where you just thought, oh--
<v Ira  Glass>在舞台上有过感觉自己老了的时候吗？你有没有想过，噢——

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:56.160
<v David  Philp>Towards the end, you really feel yourself. Because it's like a sauna up there. I mean, there's so much energy going around. And it's louder than bombs.
<v David  Philp>快结束的时候，你真的能感觉到自己的岁数。因为台上有桑拿般的热度。我是说，到处都是能量。而且声音大得像炸弹一样。

00:25:56.160 --> 00:26:06.540
<v Ira  Glass>So your wife had never seen you do this before. There must've been a part of you which felt pleased that she could see it?
<v Ira  Glass>你妻子以前从没见过你这样表演。你心里一定有一部分很高兴她能看到吧？

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:48.340
<v David Philp>Yes. I felt kind of like I'd become this other person. And when I was over there, my life over here seemed to have a sort of almost dreamlike substance. And then of course as soon as I go back, the events in October, in Japan, just began to assume that mantle of dream.
<v David Philp>是的。我觉得自己好像变成了另一个人。当我在那边时，我在这里的生活似乎带有一种近乎梦幻的实质。当然，当我一回去，10月份在日本发生的那些事情，又开始带上梦幻的色彩了。

00:26:48.340 --> 00:26:52.670
<v David Philp>I did three shows. Two in Tokyo and one in Kyoto.
<v David Philp>我做了三场演出。东京两场，京都一场。

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:55.240
<v Ira Glass>And all three, just great.
<v Ira Glass>三场都太棒了。

00:26:55.240 --> 00:27:17.090
<v David Philp>All three sold out. In Kyoto, we set a club record for the largest attendance ever. It was so packed, we couldn't actually get offstage. The only way out was over. I had to sling myself over the audience, and they carried me on their hands back through the crowd, and gently deposited me at the stage door.
<v David Philp>三场全部售罄。在京都，我们创下了俱乐部史上最高的上座率。现场太挤了，我们根本没法走下台。唯一的出路是在观众头上。我不得不把自己扔向人群，他们用手托着我穿过人群，温柔地把我放到了后台出口。

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:20.930
<v Ira  Glass>So this is your last gig? That was your last gig?
<v Ira  Glass>所以这是你的最后一场演出？那是你的最后一场？

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:23.170
<v David  Philp>Yes.
<v David  Philp>是的。

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:32.050
<v Ira Glass>And it ended with the entire audience lifting you up, and passing you bodily out, and gently depositing you out of the club?
<v Ira Glass>它以全体观众把你托起来，把你传出去，并温柔地把你送到俱乐部外面结束？

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:38.240
<v David Philp>Well, not out of the club, but to the stage door, yes. It was amazing.
<v David Philp>嗯，不是送出俱乐部，而是送到后台出口，是的。这太神奇了。

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:43.860
<v Ira Glass>I don't think I've ever really been lifted by a mob of teenagers and people in their 20s. What exactly is that like?
<v Ira Glass>我想我从没被一群青少年和20多岁的人托起来过。那到底是什么感觉？

00:27:43.860 --> 00:28:00.840
<v David   Philp>Well, in Kyoto, I felt pretty good about it. I'm not sure how I would have felt about it in London in 1977, where the scene was incredibly violent. Whenever you played, you were just as likely to get beaten up as you were to get paid.
<v David   Philp>嗯，在京都，我感觉很好。我不知道如果这事发生在1977年的伦敦我会怎么想，那里的现场极其暴力。无论你什么时候演出，被打一顿的可能性和拿到演出费的可能性一样大。

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:08.200
<v Ira Glass>Describe what it was like to come back after the tour.
<v Ira Glass>描述一下巡演后回来的感觉。

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:16.010
<v David Philp>It was hard for me to get motivated again to do my business after the tour.
<v David Philp>巡演后，我很难再有动力去经营我的业务。

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:20.910
<v Ira Glass>It just wasn't as thrilling as being on the a in front of cheering--
<v Ira Glass>那远没有在台上对着欢呼的人群——

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:49.050
<v David Philp>Well, not many things are. And it was a bit like, sort of, my dad's generation. After growing up as a kid, being fired on in World War II and all that kind of stuff. It was kind of hard getting it up for working in the shipping industry again.
<v David Philp>嗯，很少有事情能比得上那个。这有点像我父亲那一代。从小在二战中被枪林弹雨洗礼，经历那些事。再让他们回到航运业工作是很难提起劲来的。

00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:53.270
<v David Philp>Shortly after I got back, Steve Lillywhite was in town.
<v David Philp>我回来不久，史蒂夫·利利怀特就在城里。

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:54.640
<v Ira Glass>And that is?
<v Ira Glass>那是谁？

00:28:54.640 --> 00:29:08.900
<v David Philp>He was the original producer. And he was also my roommate at the time that all the Automatics stuff was going on. And now he's incredibly successful. He does, like, U2, Dave Matthews, and all that stuff.
<v David Philp>他是最初的制片人。也是“自动装置”乐队活跃时期我的室友。现在他非常成功。他做像U2、戴夫·马修斯等人的唱片。

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:18.150
<v David Philp>And anyway, he was in town, and he had some time. And so we hung out together for a couple of days. And Hunter was off. Hunter, my wife.
<v David Philp>总之，他在城里，而且有空。我们就在一起待了两天。亨特没在。亨特是我妻子。

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:41.140
<v David Philp>So we got to hang out. And we talked a little about the old days. And he told me, Big Paul from Specs does catering. And Nigel from the Members is in Australia now. And Walter from the Heartbreakers, he's a stockbroker in Manhattan.
<v David Philp>我们聚在一起，聊了聊过去的日子。他告诉我，大保罗在做餐饮，成员乐队的奈杰尔现在在澳大利亚，心碎乐队的沃尔特成了曼哈顿的证券经纪人。

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:52.590
<v David Philp>So I think I got to see, we don't get what we deserve. We get what we get. And we have to be OK with that.
<v David Philp>所以我想我明白了，我们得到的并不是我们应得的。我们得到的就是我们所得到的。我们必须学会接受。

00:29:52.590 --> 00:30:07.490
<v Ira  Glass>David Philp, lead singer of the Automatics. Though he has not quit his day job, he's been recording in the studio lately, and hopes to tour Germany in the fall.
<v Ira  Glass>“自动装置”乐队主唱戴维·菲尔普。虽然他没有辞去日常工作，但最近一直在录音室录音，并希望秋天去德国巡演。

00:30:07.490 --> 00:30:15.360
<v Ira  Glass>Why is it that the Axis powers are the ones who are going for this music right now? What is that about?
<v Ira  Glass>为什么现在的轴心国国家对这种音乐趋之若鹜？那是关于什么的？

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:30.090
<v Ira  Glass>Coming up, a famous novelist and actress from the TV show Buffy the Vampire Slayer. The impossibility of time and memory and a babysitter. It's action, action, action, believe me, in just one minute, from Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira  Glass>接下来，一位著名小说家和电视剧《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》中的演员。关于时间、记忆和保姆的不可能性。这就是动作，动作，动作，相信我，一分钟后，我们的节目从国际公共广播电台继续。

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:48.150
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Return to Childhood. Stories of people revisiting the past, what they find there, what they do not find there.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周节目里，我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来各种关于该主题的故事。今天的节目是“重返童年”。人们重温过去的故事，他们发现了什么，又没有发现什么。

00:30:48.150 --> 00:31:05.790
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at act three of our show. And this act is the story of how one of the producers of this program made a decision to return to his childhood. Alex Blumberg went searching for somebody named Susan Jordan, who he and his sister Kate and their parents knew for about a year when he was growing up in Cincinnati.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了节目的第三幕。这一幕的故事是本节目的制片人之一如何决定回归他的童年。亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格去寻找一个叫苏珊·乔丹的人，当他在辛辛那提成长时，他和他的姐姐凯特以及他们的父母认识了她大约一年。

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:26.880
<v Alex  Blumberg>These are the things that I remember about Susan Jordan. Me and her sitting in the back room, telling her about the day camp I went to that summer. I can't get myself to shut up. "And they had alligators, and snakes," I can hear myself telling her. "And this one time, this one alligator got out, and the counselor had to catch it." And on and on, like that.
<v Alex  Blumberg>这就是我关于苏珊·乔丹的记忆。我和她坐在后屋，告诉她我那天夏天参加的日营。我没法让自己停下来。“他们有鳄鱼，还有蛇，”我能听到自己告诉她，“有一次，一只鳄鱼跑出来了，管理员不得不抓住它。”就这样没完没了地说。

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:55.210
<v Alex  Blumberg>Me and Susan flipping through one of those Time Life books. Rock and Roll Through the Decades: The Sixties. She has long, brown hair. She's incredibly skinny. It's 1975. She's wearing bell bottom Levis, a faded jean jacket. She points to a picture of a bloated man in a powder blue rhinestone jumpsuit sitting cross-legged on a stage before a crowd of crying women. "That's my favorite picture of Elvis," she says. This information seems somehow personal and important.
<v Alex  Blumberg>我和苏珊翻看着其中一本《时代生活》的图书：《摇滚乐的几十年：六十年代》。她留着长长的棕色头发。她瘦得惊人。那是1975年。她穿着喇叭口的李维斯牛仔裤，一件褪色的牛仔夹克。她指着一张照片，照片上一个臃肿的男人穿着粉蓝色的莱茵石连身裤，盘腿坐在舞台上，面前是一群哭泣的女人。“那是关于猫王，我最喜欢的一张照片，”她说。这个信息似乎在某种程度上非常私密且重要。

00:31:55.210 --> 00:32:16.800
<v Alex  Blumberg>Me and Susan riding in her car. I'm going through this phase where I'm trying to notice things. So when we pull up to a stoplight, I start trying to notice the guy on the motorcycle next to us. He apparently doesn't want to be noticed, especially by a peculiar nine year old staring at him through the passenger window. "What are you looking at?" he sneers. I turn around fast and face the dashboard.
<v Alex  Blumberg>我和苏珊坐着她的车。我正处于一个试图观察事物的阶段。所以当我们停在红灯前，我开始试图观察旁边摩托车上的那个人。他显然不想被注意，尤其是不想被一个奇特的9岁男孩通过副驾驶车窗盯着看。“你在看什么？”他冷笑着说。我迅速转过身面对仪表盘。

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:21.090
<v Alex  Blumberg>"Did he say something to you?" Susan asks. "What did he say to you?"
<v Alex  Blumberg>“他跟你说了什么吗？”苏珊问。“他说了什么？”

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:24.900
<v Alex  Blumberg>"Nothing. He didn't say anything. It's fine. Look! Green light!"
<v Alex  Blumberg>“没什么。他什么也没说。没事。看！绿灯了！”

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:27.220
<v Alex  Blumberg>"Tell me what he said. What did he say to you?"
<v Alex  Blumberg>“告诉我他说了什么。他跟你说了什么？”

00:32:27.220 --> 00:32:42.120
<v Alex  Blumberg>I stay silent. I know if I tell her what he said, she'll get out of the car and try to kick his ass. Which scares me, but comforts me, too.
<v Alex  Blumberg>我保持沉默。我知道如果我告诉她他说了什么，她会下车试图踢他的屁股。这让我感到害怕，但也让我感到慰藉。

00:32:42.120 --> 00:33:01.510
<v Alex  Blumberg>Susan Jordan was our babysitter. She watched my sister and me every day after school for a couple hours until our parents got home from work. We didn't know any adults like her and we loved her. The summer before I started fifth grade, after being with us for a year, Susan got another job.
<v Alex  Blumberg>苏珊·乔丹是我们的保姆。她每天放学后负责照顾我和我姐姐几个小时，直到我们父母下班回来。我们不认识像她这样的大人，我们很爱她。就在我上五年级前的那个夏天，和我们相处了一年后，苏珊找了另一份工作。

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:20.840
<v Alex  Blumberg>The last time I saw her was Christmas Eve 1982. I'm 16, a cashier Thriftway Foods, a supermarket in Cincinnati, where I lived. The place is packed. All 25 registers are going. People are lined up halfway to the back of the store. I look up, and there's Susan Jordan.
<v Alex  Blumberg>我最后一次见到她是在1982年的平安夜。我16岁，在辛辛那提的一家叫Thriftway Foods的超市做收银员。店里挤满了人。所有25个收银台都在运作。人们排成的队伍延伸到了商店后半段。我抬头一看，是苏珊·乔丹。

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:42.240
<v Alex  Blumberg>She smiles. We talk. She doesn't have many items, so I check them through as slowly as I possibly can. I can't recall one thing we say to each other, although I remember being distinctly disappointed to hear that she's married. She hands me some kind of business card, her husband's, probably, something having to do with the building of redwood decks. She seems happy.
<v Alex  Blumberg>她微笑了。我们谈话。她没买多少东西，所以我尽量极其缓慢地把它们扫码结账。我记不清我们谈论的一件事，尽管我记得听到她结婚了时明显感到失望。她递给我一张某种名片，可能是她丈夫的，大概是关于建造红杉木甲板之类的业务。她看起来很幸福。

00:33:42.240 --> 00:34:06.990
<v Alex  Blumberg>Meanwhile there's a line of last-minute Christmas shoppers mounting behind her. I tell her to hold on, I'll try to get my break, we can catch up. She says great, and steps aside. I keep signaling to my manager, but there's no one to relieve me. Five, ten, fifteen minutes pass. I keep glancing behind at Susan, making apologetic gestures. I can still remember her standing there, holding her one bag of groceries, smiling back at me.
<v Alex  Blumberg>同时，她身后排起了最后时刻买圣诞用品的购物者队伍。我告诉她等一下，我会试着轮休，我们可以叙叙旧。她说太好了，走到了一边。我不断示意经理，但没有人能替我。五分钟、十分钟、十五分钟过去了。我不断回头看苏珊，做着道歉的手势。我至今还能想起她站在那里，手里拿着一袋杂货，对我微笑着的样子。

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:14.870
<v Alex  Blumberg>Finally she taps me on the shoulder. "I have to go," she says. "But I come in here all the time. I'm sure I'll see you around."
<v Alex  Blumberg>最后，她拍了拍我的肩膀。“我得走了，”她说，“但我经常来这儿。我相信我们还会再见的。”

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:21.710
<v Alex  Blumberg>I worked at Thriftway for two more years. I never saw Susan Jordan again.
<v Alex  Blumberg>我在Thriftway又工作了两年。我再也没见过苏珊·乔丹。

00:34:21.710 --> 00:34:40.570
<v Alex  Blumberg>It drives me crazy that I never saw her again. If I hadn't run into her at the store, I don't think I'd care. But somehow, having her play what, to me, seemed like a huge role in my life when I was a kid, and then getting just a taste of what it would be like to talk with her as a peer-- I've never forgotten that moment.
<v Alex  Blumberg>我没能再见到她这件事快把我逼疯了。如果我没在商店遇到她，我可能根本不在乎。但不知何故，她在童年时期扮演了一个对我而言似乎很重要的角色，而当我第一次尝到作为同龄人与她交谈的滋味后——我从来没有忘记那一刻。

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:49.020
<v Alex  Blumberg>I know it's ridiculous. But after years of thinking about her, imagining what she's up to, wondering if she ever thinks about me, I decide to find her.
<v Alex  Blumberg>我知道这很可笑。但在思考她多年之后，想象她的近况，想知道她是否偶尔会想起我，我决定找到她。

