<v Alex  Blumberg>That's, yeah. See, my memory is that like, she was in high school, right? She went to Withrow. But I also remember her living on her-- for some reason I remember her own house. And the reason I thought she lived by herself was we went to her house or her boyfriend's-- we had to go pick something up somewhere, and we were in her car. This big blue Duster, I think it was. And her boyfriend was there. And her boyfriend had let the cats out, and they were gone. And she was furious. And then I got in the car, and then she slammed the door, and I think we peeled out. And he was sort of standing there and trying to reason with her, and we were out of there.
<v Alex  Blumberg>那是，是的。看，我的记忆是她当时在上高中，对吧？她去了威瑟罗中学。但我还记得她独自生活——不知为什么，我记得她有自己的房子。我以为她自己住的原因是我们去过她家或者是她男友的——我们得去某个地方拿东西，我们在她的车里。那是一辆大蓝色达斯特，我想是那样。她男朋友也在。她男朋友把猫放出去了，结果猫丢了。她当时暴跳如雷。我上了车，她猛地关上门，我想我们飞驰而去。他站在那儿试图跟她讲理，而我们离开了。

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:31.410
<v Alex's Mom>Well, what did you think?
<v Alex's Mom>那么你怎么看？

00:37:31.410 --> 00:37:52.110
<v Alex  Blumberg>It made me-- I think I felt sad for her. I remember thinking-- and this is sort of in retrospect. But I think I had some sort of inkling of this idea at the time, and I'm just sort of now realizing it. But I think-- I remember thinking that he was one of the few people that she had in her life, and she couldn't even really depend on him.
<v Alex  Blumberg>这让我——我想我当时为她感到难过。我记得当时在想——这是事后追忆了。但我认为我当时就有某种潜意识的想法，而我现在才意识到。但是我认为——我记得我当时觉得他是她生命中为数不多的人之一，而她甚至不能真正依赖他。

00:37:52.110 --> 00:38:11.510
<v Alex's Mom>Yeah, you were probably right. She was a struggler. And you may have been, at that point, at that moment, her only friend. You know?
<v Alex's Mom>是的，你可能说对了。她是个在挣扎的人。而那一刻，你可能就是她唯一的朋友。你知道吗？

00:38:11.510 --> 00:38:40.530
<v Alex   Blumberg>My mom didn't have any idea where I could find Susan, which made things difficult. Because A, Susan Jordan is a very common name, and B, it's probably not her name anymore. I called the county court records department to find all the Susan Jordans married in Cincinnati. My mom asked a friend who worked for the city to search all the Cincinnati birth records. I contacted high school alumni associations. I asked friends at high-powered newspapers to run background checks.
<v Alex   Blumberg>我妈根本不知道去哪能找到苏珊，这让事情变得很困难。因为第一，苏珊·乔丹是一个非常常见的名字，第二，这很可能已经不是她的名字了。我打电话给县法院记录部门查找所有在辛辛那提结婚的苏珊·乔丹。我妈让我一个在市政府工作的朋友搜索了所有辛辛那提的出生记录。我联系了高中校友会。我让在高级报社工作的朋友进行了背景调查。

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:52.170
<v Alex   Blumberg>Finally, there was one former Susan Jordan who stood out. She seemed the right age. She was married, living in a Cincinnati suburb. She had a couple of kids. Her husband was a lawyer. I got her number from information.
<v Alex   Blumberg>最后，有一个前苏珊·乔丹引起了我的注意。她年龄似乎正好。她结了婚，住在一个辛辛那提郊区。她有两个孩子。她丈夫是名律师。我通过查询得到了她的号码。

00:38:52.170 --> 00:39:04.620
<v Alex   Blumberg>And it wasn't until I sat down to call her that it hit me. A phone call from someone you babysat 20 years ago might not be a welcome surprise, but in fact strange and creepy. Here I am practicing sounding benign.
<v Alex   Blumberg>直到我坐下来给她打电话，我才意识到。一个20年前当过你保姆的人打来的电话可能不是什么惊喜，事实上反倒显得奇怪和诡异。我在这儿练习着让声音听起来温和一点。

00:39:04.620 --> 00:39:15.370
<v Alex Blumberg>One, two, one, two. Susan. Is this Susan Jordan? Is this Susan Jordan? Oh god, oh god.
