00:44:21.820 --> 00:44:23.110
<v Susan Jordan>You're so lucky.
<v Susan Jordan>你太幸运了。

00:44:23.110 --> 00:44:48.970
<v Alex  Blumberg>It's funny, because when I-- like, you know, I remember these very particular incidents. And that was one of them. And probably the reason I remember it is because it seemed very significant to you. I think I sensed, as a kid, that it was really upsetting to you because it-- I think I felt at that time that you didn't really have very many people in your life, at that point, who you could trust.
<v Alex  Blumberg>这很有趣，因为当我也——你知道，我记得那些非常具体的事件。那是其中之一。我记得它的原因可能就是因为它对你来说似乎意义重大。我想我在孩提时代感觉到，那对你来说真的很令人不安，因为它——我想我当时觉得那时候你生命中并没有多少你可以信任的人。

00:44:48.970 --> 00:45:13.610
<v Susan Jordan>Oh, I didn't have hardly anybody. My whole family moved out of town. I had no family at all. I moved out the day I graduated from high school. And I was 17, because I started a year early. I just wanted out. And see, I had found out that I got the scholarship. I packed up that night.
<v Susan Jordan>噢，我几乎没什么人能信任。我的全家都搬出城了。我一个家人都没有。我高中毕业那天就搬走了。我当时才17岁，因为我提前一年上学。我只是想出去。你看，我当时发现我拿到了奖学金。那天晚上我就收拾好行李了。

00:45:13.610 --> 00:45:19.140
<v Alex Blumberg>Why did you want to get out so bad?
<v Alex Blumberg>你为什么那么想出去？

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:30.380
<v Susan Jordan>Because my family was dysfunctional. But my mom-- it was pretty bad.
<v Susan Jordan>因为我的家庭功能失调。但我妈——情况很糟糕。

00:45:30.380 --> 00:45:52.340
<v Susan Jordan>The girl that I lived with-- at that time, she was taking a lot of drugs. And her boyfriend. And every time I would come home, they would always try to get me to take drugs with them or something. And I really didn't do it much at all. And it was really tough to come home.
<v Susan Jordan>和我住在一起的那个女孩——当时，她吸了很多毒。还有她男朋友。每次我回家，他们总是试图拉我一起吸毒之类的。我真的没怎么做。回家真的很艰难。

00:45:52.340 --> 00:46:04.670
<v Susan Jordan>And I guess I must have been suffering a little bit. I really missed my little brothers and my little sister, and they were gone. And I was, I guess, maybe trying to substitute.
<v Susan Jordan>我猜我当时肯定有点痛苦。我真的很想念我的小弟弟小妹妹们，他们都离开了。而我，我猜，也许在试图找人替代。

00:46:04.670 --> 00:46:17.230
<v Alex Blumberg>I think maybe that's one of the reasons that I remember, that we remember you so fondly, though. Because I think it worked both ways. I think that we felt-- that that did make you feel closer to us. I think that we responded.
<v Alex Blumberg>我觉得这可能就是为什么我们那么怀念你，因为我认为这是双向的。我觉得我们感受到了——那确实让你感觉和我们更亲近。我想我们做出了回应。

00:46:17.230 --> 00:46:28.110
<v Susan Jordan>Well, I was desparately-- I guess I was looking for a family. But I mean, if only you knew, you probably wouldn't have hired me. But I mean, people are complicated.
<v Susan Jordan>嗯，我当时非常——我猜我在寻找一个家。但是我是说，如果你们知道真相，你们可能就不会雇我了。但是我是说，人都是复杂的。

00:46:28.110 --> 00:46:46.060
<v Susan Jordan>Now, what I really wanted to do was spend more time with your mom and dad. But I was terrified. I mean, I just couldn't do it. I was too shy. So a lot of times-- I thought they were asking me to stay longer and talk and I would just run out.
<v Susan Jordan>现在，我真正想做的是花更多时间和你父母在一起。但我很害怕。我是说，我就是做不到。我太害羞了。所以很多时候——我觉得他们是想让我多待一会儿聊聊，但我只是直接跑了。

00:46:46.060 --> 00:46:47.740
<v Alex Blumberg>I'm sure they were.
<v Alex Blumberg>我敢肯定他们是的。

00:46:47.740 --> 00:46:51.470
<v Susan Jordan>And they probably thought, what's wrong with her? But I just couldn't do it.
<v Susan Jordan>他们可能在想，她怎么了？但我就是做不到。

00:46:51.470 --> 00:46:55.190
<v Alex Blumberg>So you sort of talked to us instead, sounds like.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以听起来你反倒是跟我们交流更多。

00:46:55.190 --> 00:47:02.620
<v Susan Jordan>Yeah. I was comfortable around kids because I had kids in my family.
<v Susan Jordan>是的。我和孩子在一起很自在，因为我家里就有孩子。

00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:22.740
<v Alex  Blumberg>Every time the subject of her hard times came up, I'd hear a subtle hesitancy in Susan's voice. At first I thought it was embarrassment, but that wasn't it, exactly. It wasn't until we'd been talking for hours that I realized what it was.
<v Alex  Blumberg>每当她艰难时期的主题出现，我就能在苏珊的声音里听到一种细微的犹豫。起初我以为是尴尬，但并不完全是这样。直到我们谈了几个小时后，我才意识到那是什么。

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:37.800
<v Alex  Blumberg>She was waiting for the other shoe to drop. She hasn't forgotten that her past had happened. She'd just forgotten that I'd witnessed part of it. And her fear became clear, the one that had been gnawing at her our entire conversation, was that I was calling to say she damaged me by exposing me to it.
<v Alex  Blumberg>她在等着另一只鞋掉下来。她没忘记她的过去曾经发生过。她只是忘记了我曾见证过其中的一部分。她的恐惧变得清晰起来，那个在她整个谈话过程中一直啃噬着她的恐惧，就是我打电话来是为了说她通过让我接触那些经历而伤害了我。

00:47:37.800 --> 00:48:09.440
<v Susan Jordan>I don't think I was too kind back then. Because there was a lot of turmoil in my life and in my family. And that's what my fear is, that I might have had some kind of negative impact on people. And I know probably I did, on a couple people. But they were my age. But you just want to remember, yeah, I was the babysitter. The kids loved me. Blah blah blah. I would be devastated if I heard anything different.
<v Susan Jordan>我那时候不太善良。因为我的生活和家庭里有很多动荡。而这就是我的恐惧，我可能给别人带来了一些负面影响。我知道我可能确实对几个人产生了影响。但他们也是我这个年纪。但你只想记得，是的，我是那个保姆。孩子们爱我。叽里呱啦。如果我听到任何不同的说法，我会崩溃的。

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:23.740
<v Alex    Blumberg>There are parts of your past you don't want to go back to, parts of yourself you don't want to go back to. And for Susan Jordan, the year of her life that I remember is the year she'd just as soon forget.
<v Alex    Blumberg>你过去的一些部分你不想回去，你过去的一些自我你不想回去。对于苏珊·乔丹来说，我记得的那一年，是她宁愿忘记的一年。

00:48:23.740 --> 00:48:36.950
<v Alex    Blumberg>And it turns out, I had also done my best to forget what I was like that year. I didn't think of myself this way at all, but Susan Jordan reminded me in the gentlest terms possible. When I was nine, I was anxious and bookish. I was kind of uptight.
<v Alex    Blumberg>事实证明，我也尽了最大努力去忘记那一年我是什么样的。我根本没把自己想成那样，但苏珊·乔丹以最温柔的方式提醒了我。9岁时，我很焦虑，书呆子气。我很拘谨。

00:48:36.950 --> 00:49:22.650
<v Susan Jordan>Not to seem as an insult, but I just kept thinking, these kids don't know how to play. When I went to your rooms, it didn't seem like you had a whole lot of toys. I hope I've got this right. But it just seemed like there were mostly books and more educational things. I mean, I remember you had planets in your room and a chemistry set. And I didn't remember that Kate had hardly any dolls. You didn't seem quite as playful as other kids that I had babysat. Just more serious in general. So mainly, I think that's what I did, was try to play.
<v Susan Jordan>并不是要侮辱，但我只是不停地想，这些孩子怎么都不会玩啊。当我走进你们的房间，看起来你们并没有很多玩具。希望我没记错。但似乎大多是书和教育类东西。我是说，我记得你们房里有行星模型和化学实验套件。我不记得凯特有什么洋娃娃。你们看起来不像我照顾过的其他孩子那样爱玩。总的来说更严肃一点。所以我想，我主要做的就是试图去玩。

00:49:22.650 --> 00:49:57.100
<v Alex    Blumberg>Perhaps the most amazing thing about this whole story is how little our memories have deceived us about each other, even if they had deceived us about ourselves. As Susan said at one point, each of us remembered what we needed to about the other. I needed to remember the part of Susan that she doesn't think about much. Her toughness in the face of hardship. She said she mostly remembered a side of my family that I just take for granted-- that it was calm in our house. That there were books. There wasn't much fighting.
<v Alex    Blumberg>关于整个故事，最奇妙的事情也许是我们对彼此的记忆有多么没有欺骗我们，即使我们在对自己这部分有过欺骗。正如苏珊曾说过的那样，我们每个人都记住了关于对方所需要记住的东西。我需要记住苏珊不怎么想的那些部分。她在逆境面前的坚韧。她说她主要记得我家的一面，那种我视为理所当然的一面——我们家很安静。有很多书。没怎么打架。

00:49:57.100 --> 00:50:09.430
<v Susan Jordan>It was the first time in my life where I had ever seen that people lived differently than the way I lived. And that's what I decided I wanted for myself.
<v Susan Jordan>那是我一生中第一次看到人们的生活方式与我不同。那就是我决定为自己争取的东西。

00:50:09.430 --> 00:50:35.720
<v Alex Blumberg>You can try to return to childhood by looking at photos, or visiting the old neighborhood, or listening to recordings. Or you can find someone who knew you back then, someone you haven't seen since. They still carry within themselves a picture of you that's unclouded by the years in between. They'll remember you better than you remember yourself, and you can do the same thing for them.
<v Alex Blumberg>你可以通过查看照片、访问旧街区或听录音来重返童年。或者你可以找到一个当年认识你的人，一个你后来再没见过的人。他们身上依然带着一张未被中间岁月所模糊的关于你的照片。他们会比你自己更好地记住你，你也能为他们做同样的事。

00:50:35.720 --> 00:50:39.060
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格。

00:50:39.060 --> 00:51:18.490
<v Ira Glass>Act Four. Every Day I Forget Something Else.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕：我每天都在遗忘。

00:51:18.490 --> 00:51:42.050
<v Ira Glass>Let's end our program today with one of the ideas we began it with. Remember Kayla, the seventh greater who is already aware of how she cannot remember her own past? Well, this very idea comes up in a novel by Nicholson Baker, a really great and unusual novel called The Everlasting Story of Nory. The book is told from the point of view of a nine year old named Nory who, at some point, talks about how hard it is to remember things. Actress Michelle Trachtenberg reads a brief passage for us.
<v Ira Glass>今天让我们以我们开始时的想法来结束节目。还记得凯拉吗？那个已经意识到自己无法记住过去七年的七年级学生？这个想法出现在尼科尔森·贝克的一本小说里，一本非常伟大且独特的小说，叫《诺里的永恒故事》。这本书是从一个叫诺里的9岁女孩的角度讲述的，她在某个时候谈到了记住事情有多难。演员米歇尔·特拉亨伯格为我们读了一段。

00:51:42.050 --> 00:52:06.190
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>That afternoon, Nory tried to reconstruct every tiny detail of the International Chinese School in her mind. She couldn't even remember all the kids in the class. She remembered one very nice girl named Stephie, who left later on, who had a birthday party at her swimming pool where Nory had floundered into the deep end and had gotten about a gallon and a half of water in her lungs and thrown up a tiny bit on the grass.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>那天下午，诺里试图在脑海中重建国际中文学校的每一个微小细节。她甚至记不起班上所有的孩子。她记得一个叫史蒂芬妮的非常好的女孩，她后来离开了，在史蒂芬妮家的游泳池办过生日派对，当时诺里曾扑腾进了深水区，肺里大概呛了一加仑半的水，还在草地上吐了一点点。

00:52:06.190 --> 00:52:22.470
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>She gave Stephie a pair of tiny glass slippers wrapped up in probably the best wrapping paper she had ever drawn. She still thought about those glass slippers. They were paperweights that a glassblower made, but they worked as real doll shoes. They were amazingly wonderful.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>她给了史蒂芬妮一对小玻璃拖鞋，包在她画过的最好的包装纸里。她依然经常想起那双玻璃拖鞋。它们是玻璃吹制工做的镇纸，但确实能当真正的玩偶鞋穿。它们美妙得令人惊叹。

00:52:22.470 --> 00:52:43.660
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>It disturbed Nory very much to think that all she was going to know about what happened in her life was not very much at all. You can only really remember the things that happened when you were an older child and the things that happened to you now. That is, yesterday, or the day before yesterday, or late last week.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>想到她余生能知道的关于自己经历的事情其实寥寥无几，这让诺里感到非常不安。你只能真正记住你作为大孩子时发生的事情，以及现在发生在你身上的事情。也就是昨天，或者前天，或者是上周晚些时候。

00:52:43.660 --> 00:53:09.520
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>You live your life always in the present, and even in the present, this day, dozens and hundreds of little tiny things happen. So many that by the end of the day, you can't make a list of them. You lose track of them unless something reminds you. Say someone says, remember when you dropped your ruler this morning? And you do remember. But then that is lost in the tangle.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>你总是生活在当下，即使是在当下，这一天也会发生几十、几百件小事。太多了，以至于到了一天结束时，你无法列出清单。除非有什么东西提醒你，否则你会失去对它们的追踪。比如有人说，还记得你今天早上弄掉尺子的时候吗？你就记得了。但随后那又消失在错综复杂的记忆里。

00:53:09.520 --> 00:53:24.520
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>Now, some things you can just accept that you're not going to have the slightest chance of remembering. It would be nice, but you know that it would be basically impossible. For instance, being in your mother's womb, as it's called. Some people thought babies could remember that.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>当然，有些事情你可以坦然接受，你是绝不可能记住的。如果能记住当然好，但你知道那基本上是不可能的。比如所谓的在母亲的子宫里。有些人以为婴儿能记得那个。

00:53:24.520 --> 00:53:51.240
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>Nory one morning asked Littleguy if he could remember being tucked away in mommy's belly long ago, and he said, "Yes." He said, "It had all things there, in she's tummy. It had things that were called steam trains. It was filled with they. Filled with steam trains, city of Truro, Lord of the Isles, the Mallard. Pictures with steam trains, and toy ones, and jumping things. Filled, filled with they."
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>有一天早上，诺里问小家伙他是否还能记得很久以前蜷缩在妈咪肚子里，他说：“记得。”他说：“那里什么都有，在她的肚子里。有叫蒸汽火车的东西。到处都是。充满了它们。充满蒸汽火车，特鲁罗城，岛屿之主，野鸭号。照片里有蒸汽火车，玩具火车，还有跳动的物体。充满，充满了它们。”

00:53:51.240 --> 00:54:12.830
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>Well, of course there weren't toy trains in Nory's mother's womb, unless maybe he was a remembering the small intestine chuffing around. Maybe he was remembering a freight train of food being digested, going around and around him. But probably not.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>好吧，当然诺里的母亲子宫里没有玩具火车，除非也许他在记得小肠在呼哧呼哧地蠕动。也许他记得的是一列食物运送的货运列车正在消化中绕着他转。但很可能不是。

00:54:12.830 --> 00:54:50.050
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>Still, Nory thought it would be nice if you could think back at least to the age of three. It shouldn't be impossible. Three was older than Littleguy, and Littleguy could understand an amazing number of things. But Nory couldn't go back that far, really, except for a few scribs and scraps. She remembered being eight, and back into being seven, and she went pretty much back to five, and then it teetered a little bit. She only remembered her fourth birthday party, a mermaid party, because she had watched the tape of it a number of times on TV.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>不过，诺里还是觉得如果能回想到3岁大就好了。那不应该是不可能的。3岁比小家伙大，小家伙都能理解惊人数量的事情。但诺里真的回不到那么久以前，除了一些零碎的记忆。她记得8岁，追溯到7岁，她基本上能回到5岁，然后就开始摇摇欲坠了。她只记得她4岁生日派对，那个美人鱼主题派对，因为她在电视上看过很多次派对的录像。

00:54:50.050 --> 00:55:20.930
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>One thing, though, she made a point of remembering and passing onto her older self. Every year she got a year older, she said to her parents, "Remember when I was five, I said I was five going on six? Remember when I was six, I said six going on seven? And when I was seven, I'd be going seven on eight. And then going on eight, on nine? Well, now I'm going nine on ten." So each year, the list of years got a little longer. But she remembered the earlier times that way by saying the list over.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>不过，有一件事她确实设法记住并传递给年长的自己。每年她大一岁，她就会对父母说：“还记得我5岁时说我5岁快6岁了吗？记得我6岁时说6岁快7岁吗？7岁时我会说7岁快8岁？然后快8岁，9岁？好吧，现在我是9岁快10岁了。”所以每年，年份清单都会长一点点。但她通过不断重复那个清单，记住了早先的时光。

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:56.430
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>Another thing she made sure to bring along every year with her for a long time was the memory that there were many, many little amounts of money that she had found in the car and thought could be hers, but maybe not. Or times her parents had bought her a doll outfit or something when she told him she would reimburse them later, when they got home, with her own money. Or gifts she bought other people with her own money. But borrowing it from her parents, since she'd forgotten her purse. She would skip a week, not thinking to it, then still remember it, and bring it into the next week. Then skip a week, then bring it over.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>她每年很长一段时间里确保随身带着的另一件事是，她曾在车里发现过很多很多少量的钱，以为那些钱可能是她的，但也可能不是。或者她的父母曾给她买过一套玩偶服，而当她告诉父母回家后会用自己的钱还给他们时。或者她用自己的钱给别人买礼物。但既然她忘记了钱包，她是从父母那里借的钱。她会跳过一周，不往那方面想，然后依然记得它，把它带到下一周。然后再跳过一周，然后再带过去。

00:55:56.430 --> 00:56:17.950
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>Finally, she couldn't keep the amount in her head, because it had been added onto and subtracted from so much. And it began to pull at her. And she thought, "I know. I'll pay them $100 when I grow up, and that will surely make up for anything I've borrowed along the way. Then she didn't have to keep track of that.
<v Michelle  Trachtenberg>最后，她无法在脑海里记下那个数额，因为它被加加减减了太多次。它开始让她感到困扰。她想：“我知道了。我长大后会还他们100美元，这肯定能抵消我一路借来的任何东西。那样我就不用追踪它了。”

00:56:17.950 --> 00:56:33.830
<v Ira Glass>Michelle Trachtenberg, who plays Buffy's little sister and a portal and a key on the TV show Buffy the Vampire Slayer, reading an except from Nicholson Baker's Everlasting Story of Nory.
<v Ira Glass>米歇尔·特拉亨伯格，在《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》中饰演巴菲的妹妹、通道和钥匙的演员，阅读尼科尔森·贝克的《诺里的永恒故事》片段。

00:56:33.830 --> 00:56:59.670
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Jonathan Goldstein and myself with Alex Blumberg, Blue Chevigny, Starlee Kine, Erin Yanke, and Annie Baxter. Senior producer Julie Snyder, consigliere Sarah Vowell, production help from Todd Bachmann.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们的节目今天由乔纳森·戈尔茨坦和我制作，亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格、蓝·切维尼、斯塔莉·凯恩、艾琳·扬克和安妮·巴克斯特参与。高级制片人朱莉·斯奈德，顾问萨拉·沃威尔，托德·巴赫曼提供制作帮助。

